00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:00.970
Since then we've also
released Cloud Functions,

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:03.910
which is a-- excuse
me-- functions

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.600
as a service-based product.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.690
And these two together
have allowed developers

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.960
to understand the benefits
of serverless compute,

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.008
allowing them to
focus on their code,

00:01:13.008 --> 00:01:14.800
and not worry about
the infrastructure that

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.390
is necessary to run that code.

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.820
Our customers, probably
all of you here today,

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:22.370
or many of you here today,
have been very successful

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:24.650
using these platforms
to run your business,

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.020
and recognizing the value
that App Engine and Cloud

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.240
Functions provide
their engineering teams

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.540
on lowering the requirements
of running production code.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.870
We've even heard people
say, Google is my SRE.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:39.730
I don't have to worry about
having that role in my company.

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:42.730
But serverless wasn't always
accessible to everybody.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.550
There were restrictions
and limitations

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.653
on using our serverless
platforms that caused problems.

00:01:48.653 --> 00:01:50.320
And we often heard
from customers things

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.690
like I have these
existing binaries that I

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.760
want to be able
to use, or there's

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:57.430
a particular coding language
that is optimal for the task

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:58.870
that I'm using.

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:00.460
When is that going
to be supported?

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.700
Or even Java developers maybe.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.765
Python developers
probably remember.

00:02:05.765 --> 00:02:07.390
Maybe it wasn't even
the whole language

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:09.307
that was available in
the serverless platform.

00:02:09.307 --> 00:02:11.950
It was just a subset of it.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:13.660
So there were challenges.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:17.050
And we wanted to make this more
accessible to everybody, not

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:20.120
just the people who could
fit the mold that we had.

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:21.820
But what is serverless?

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:24.130
How do we think of it as Google?

00:02:24.130 --> 00:02:26.380
After all of the years of
running App Engine and Cloud

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:29.380
Functions, all of the
feedback from customers,

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:32.350
we've developed a model for
how we think about serverless.

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.490
And it divides into two pieces.

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:36.130
One is the operational model.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:38.410
This is the sort of way
that serverless runs.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.578
It's the idea that you
don't have to worry

00:02:40.578 --> 00:02:41.620
about the infrastructure.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:44.740
There's no servers to
provision, no operating systems

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:47.680
to install, none of the
sort of details of how

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.420
things work at that level.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.010
It's also about
managed security.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:52.840
So ensuring that
Google takes care

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:56.378
of the details on how to secure
your production environment.

00:02:56.378 --> 00:02:58.420
And then a really important
piece for many of you

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:01.390
is pay as you go, or
pay only for usage.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:04.210
The idea that you aren't
paying for provisioned servers

00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:06.730
or provisioned nodes, but
only the compute that you're

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:09.310
actually using.

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:11.660
And there's a programming
model aspect to this as well.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:13.690
So this is the
developer experience.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:17.050
It's moving from being
a traditional model

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:18.550
to being more
service-based, being

00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:21.160
able to have managed
services that you leverage

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.245
to get your work done
instead of bringing out

00:03:23.245 --> 00:03:26.300
your own dependencies or even
writing those things yourself.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.120
It's about being event-driven.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.520
So instead of building
monolithic applications that

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:32.620
are focused on the
application, think about

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.330
how the data flows
through your system,

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:36.490
and how you can attach
compute to the events that

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:38.392
are generated by that.

00:03:38.392 --> 00:03:40.600
And then there's a third
piece that we think it truly

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:42.790
means to be a
serverless at Google,

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:46.215
and that is about being open.

00:03:46.215 --> 00:03:48.340
It's really the ideal of
what serverless should be,

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.390
not so much what serverless
has been in the past.

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:52.140
So we want to make
sure that the developer

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:53.340
experience is idiomatic.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.850
It feels natural to
you as a developer.

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:59.090
You're not constrained by what
the platform has to offer.

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:00.610
And also that it
should be portable.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:03.670
You don't want to be locked into
a particular platform vendor

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:05.578
when you're using a
serverless technology.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:07.870
You should be able to run it
wherever it makes the most

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:09.953
sense for your business
and change your mind later

00:04:09.953 --> 00:04:11.350
on if you want to.

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:13.030
This is the true
value of serverless,

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:14.380
all of these things.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.329
So that you can just
focus on writing the code

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.430
that matters, the core
value of your business,

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:20.513
and not have to worry about
all the other details.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:25.030
Separately, though, from
serverless happening,

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.100
there's been another
trend in the industry;

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.260
the rise of containers.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:31.150
Containers over the
last four or five years

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:33.010
have really picked up.

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:35.890
This is a Google Trends graph
showing like peak interest

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:37.540
at the top, at 100.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:41.230
You can see we're at the current
peak interest for containers.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:42.970
Containers, if you
don't what they are,

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:45.970
are a way to easily
move code and binaries

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:47.963
between environments.

00:04:47.963 --> 00:04:50.380
So you've got the confidence
that the way that application

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:53.800
runs in one environment will
carry over to the next place

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:54.797
that you run it.

00:04:54.797 --> 00:04:57.130
So you're going to have a
CICD system produce containers

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.140
for you, validate
that they work,

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:01.810
and then literally take the
same code, the same binaries,

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:05.080
the same container package
and run it in production,

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:07.310
and get all the same experience.

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:09.880
In fact, it's become
such a great experience

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:12.400
for developers and operators
alike that it is now

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.060
an industry standard with a
ton of tooling and support

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:18.550
and an entire ecosystem
around how you build and use

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:20.287
containers.

00:05:20.287 --> 00:05:22.370
And while you can containerize
just about anything

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:25.780
and the tools make this really
easy, running containers,

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:27.270
eh, not always so easy.

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:30.290
There's orchestration platforms
that you need to deal with.

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:31.960
There's Kubernetes.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:35.110
There's all of the APIs
and lifecycle management

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:35.890
that go into that.

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:38.990
You have to think about how
to scale your application,

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:41.440
how to get the most out of
it with all of the stuff.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:43.992
It really means that you
need an operator running--

00:05:43.992 --> 00:05:45.700
sitting there with
your code to make sure

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:48.390
that it's running
correctly in production.

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:51.910
But Steren, what if there was a
way to do all of this greatness

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:54.880
with containers and get all
the benefits out of this

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:56.710
without the need
for an operator?

00:05:56.710 --> 00:05:59.000
What if developers could
do it on their own?

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.157
STEREN GIANNINI: I wonder,
what if it could be easier?

00:06:02.157 --> 00:06:02.740
You know what?

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.270
Let's switch to the demo screen.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.110
What if I could
simply from the Cloud

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:16.500
console open the
Cloud Run section?

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:20.210
What if I could click a button?

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:25.820
The deployment form opens, and
paste the URL of a container

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:27.980
image that I have
stored previously

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:30.830
