00:07:24.994 --> 00:07:25.956
Thank you.

00:07:25.956 --> 00:07:30.290
[APPLAUSE]

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:33.920
But you might wonder, what
can I do with Cloud Run?

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:39.880
Well, although the use
cases are very broad,

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:45.810
Cloud Run allows you to deploy
any stateless HTTP container.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:50.190
So that means that any HTTP
request-driven workload

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.800
or workload that you can
invoke asynchronously

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:56.550
will be a good
fit for Cloud Run.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:08:02.010
This kind of platform allows
a wide variety of use cases.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:03.750
Let's take a look
at some of them.

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:07.570
We can find two categories.

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:11.040
The first one is what
we call public services.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:14.640
They would be you want to host
a website automatically scaled

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:16.470
to handle the incoming traffic.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.060
Cloud Run is great for that.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:21.225
If you want to expose an
API endpoint, the same.

00:08:21.225 --> 00:08:24.970
The back end for your
mobile app, Cloud Run.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:28.050
Or maybe a simple
web hook that you

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:32.400
use to receive a GitHub
notification or maybe

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:33.720
a chat bot.

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:35.309
This is also a good use case.

00:08:35.309 --> 00:08:37.500
Everything that is
automatically scaled

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:42.780
to handle the incoming
traffic that varies over time.

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:46.080
Then there is a second
category of services;

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:49.710
those who are not
exposed to the internet.

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:53.010
Those ones are, for example,
if you are breaking out

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:54.940
the monolith of
your application,

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:57.600
you might have created
many microservices.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:00.570
Well, what if each of
those microservices

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:02.940
could scale
independently on demand

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:06.010
to meet the load that
is needed from them?

00:09:06.010 --> 00:09:08.010
So private
microservices are also

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:10.590
a good fit; things that are
not exposed to the internet,

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:14.910
but that are part of your
overall cloud architecture.

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:17.730
And lastly, as I
mentioned, you can

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:20.550
take those private services,
but instead of invoking them

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:23.370
directly via HTTP,
you can use things

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:27.030
like Web Server or Cloud
Tasks to do asynchronous task

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:31.050
execution where the task is
executed on an on demand Cloud

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:32.260
Run service.

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:37.350
So some of our alpha testers
have built many such use cases

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:41.370
for doing image transformation,
video processing, document

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:43.440
processing.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.050
All of this is feasible
with Cloud Run.

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:49.720
So over this talk, we
are going to go over

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:54.410
the three main pillars
of the Cloud Run product.

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:57.290
We will see how it
takes containers,

00:09:57.290 --> 00:09:59.540
and how it deploys
them very fast.

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:03.680
We will explain a bit what are
the benefits of containers.

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:09.690
We will show you how this is
a natively serverless product.

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:11.630
And lastly, you
will see that you

00:10:11.630 --> 00:10:14.300
can have the same Cloud
Run experience wherever

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.870
you want it.

00:10:16.870 --> 00:10:18.110
So let's get started.

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:20.920
Containers to
production in seconds.

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:23.500
This is the first
pillar of Cloud Run.

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:28.150
So yes, Cloud Run
runs containers.

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:32.110
But why containers?

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:36.280
Well, first off, they allow
you to run any programming

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:37.420
language.

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:40.660
So you're not restricted anymore
by the five or seven languages

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:43.090
that Google Cloud provides.

00:10:43.090 --> 00:10:43.660
No, no, no.

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:45.790
Any language you want,
you can bring in.

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:48.690
I brought a shell
script into Cloud Run,

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:50.630
if that is even a language.

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:51.870
But really anything.

00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:52.900
We've got [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:54.250
We've got Rest.

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:56.230
You can try that.

00:10:56.230 --> 00:10:59.630
Along with your favorite
programming language,

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:03.680
you can also use your favorite
library from this language,

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:06.880
but also from the operating
system, because containers also

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:13.720
capture the OS packages that
you want inside your container.

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:17.350
And more interestingly,
any software, any binary

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:20.200
can also go into your container.

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:22.990
And so this unlocks some
very interesting use cases

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:25.030
where it's not only
about source code.

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:30.310
It's about executing
software in a serverless way.

00:11:30.310 --> 00:11:35.330
Containers have a very
large ecosystem around them.

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:39.700
For example, you will find
many, many official base images

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:42.460
on Docker Hub that are
officially maintained

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:45.580
by the language maintainers.

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:50.380
And we have to acknowledge that
today containers have become

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:52.810
an industry standard
for packaging,

00:11:52.810 --> 00:11:56.100
distributing, testing,
and deploying software.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:59.170
They are really, in my opinion,
better than a zip file.

