we also have several
constellations

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:35.900
of optical and radar
satellites that we launch,

00:12:35.900 --> 00:12:38.993
operate, and provide services
around the data that's

00:12:38.993 --> 00:12:40.160
produced from these sensors.

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:47.300
So let's talk about
serverless computing.

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:51.530
As you can see from these
vintage photos, our use of IT

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:54.620
goes quite far back.

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:57.380
We have actually more than
30 years of experience

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:01.460
in storing, processing, and
distributing digital satellite

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:02.320
imagery.

00:13:06.550 --> 00:13:10.330
The nature of these
several year long programs

00:13:10.330 --> 00:13:13.180
when launching a
spacecraft obviously amount

00:13:13.180 --> 00:13:16.990
to quite a large amount
of stable and legacy code

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:18.570
and infrastructure.

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:21.010
But what we started our
move to public cloud

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:23.740
platforms a few years ago.

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:25.930
In that perspective,
I was very excited

00:13:25.930 --> 00:13:27.790
when Steren proposed
us to be part

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:32.380
of the alpha tester program for
Cloud Run to experiment ways

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:36.490
in which we could shift
our usage of compute power

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:37.750
for this use case.

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:45.880
As part of our move
to cloud platforms,

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:49.480
we recently opened the
OneAtlas digital platform

00:13:49.480 --> 00:13:55.660
to provide data use API
access to tasking satellites

00:13:55.660 --> 00:13:59.600
to acquire imagery when
specific conditions are met,

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:04.840
and the time when those weather
conditions are recurrent.

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:07.600
We provide also download
and viewing access

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:10.660
to raw and processed imagery.

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:15.760
And we also provide extract
analytics from these raw data

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:20.350
and provide insights such as
vehicle and infrastructure

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:25.360
detection, change monitoring,
precision agriculture,

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:28.590
or deforestation monitoring.

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:31.310
Now, fueling this
platform actually

00:14:31.310 --> 00:14:35.150
requires ingesting thousands
of images every day.

00:14:35.150 --> 00:14:37.220
Each one of those
images is composed

00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:40.610
of several billions of
pixels, and weighing

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:45.240
in at several gigabytes
of raw compressed data.

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:49.170
Under the scenes this leads
to quite a few challenges.

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:52.380
We want to minimize
storage size,

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:56.520
and we want to efficiently
respond to undermined access

00:14:56.520 --> 00:14:59.000
to provide services
without overprovisioning

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:00.378
compute instances.

00:15:04.130 --> 00:15:07.060
So let's explore a scenario
where Cloud Run can help us

00:15:07.060 --> 00:15:09.080
with that.

00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:12.590
For this use case, we'll be
transforming our raw imagery

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:15.150
into the growing
industry standard that's

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:19.080
called the Cloud-Optimized
GeoTIFFs, which is just

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:23.030
a standard TIFF file, but where
all the imagery and metadata

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:25.190
and the pixel indexes
have been stored

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:29.360
at the beginning of the file
to be compatible with object

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:33.720
storage Get Range requests.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:36.240
So our first step
is a batch process

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:38.910
which will ingest
our raw imagery

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:43.545
and transform it into these
Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFF files

00:15:43.545 --> 00:15:46.320
into a cloud storage bucket.

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:49.670
Jobs like these will be
continuously launched whenever

00:15:49.670 --> 00:15:53.680
a new image becomes available.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:55.470
So now in the second
phase in order

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:58.410
to be able to service
requests to clients

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:03.300
who want to only access
parts of the imagery,

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:05.670
we can deploy
Cloud Run services,

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:10.320
which are just local images that
contain any low level code that

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:13.140
will be able to
access and transform

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:16.350
the raw pixel data
from the GeoTIFF files,

00:16:16.350 --> 00:16:20.370
and to fit the needs of
analytics jobs, machine

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:22.320
learning, training,
and inference jobs,

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:24.360
or just interactive viewing.

00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:32.010
So why is Cloud Run a great
fit for this use case?

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:36.390
Being able to run custom
code on the serving instances

00:16:36.390 --> 00:16:41.550
allows us to store the data
only once in a private format

00:16:41.550 --> 00:16:44.610
while still offering the
full range of services

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:46.590
at request time.

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:50.670
And this lowers
recurring storage costs

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:54.490
by avoiding duplication of data.

00:16:54.490 --> 00:16:58.050
We also take advantage of the
scaling down to zero instances

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:03.050
to be able to deploy as
many services as we need.

00:17:03.050 --> 00:17:05.869
And the scaling up
of the instances

00:17:05.869 --> 00:17:11.060
also ensures us that our
services are available and will

00:17:11.060 --> 00:17:14.079
absorb any usage likes.

00:17:14.079 --> 00:17:19.119
Now, in situations where using
public cloud isn't feasible,

00:17:19.119 --> 00:17:22.579
the Knative roots that
Cloud Run is built upon

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:27.720
will allow for code reuse
and shield us from lock in.

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:31.500
So yeah, all in all, we
are excited to continue

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:34.740
exploring use cases that can
be unlocked by Cloud Run.

00:17:34.740 --> 00:17:41.430
And we just can't wait for
it to be available in Europe.

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:43.720
STEREN GIANNINI: It's coming.

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:44.430
Thanks, Thomas.

00:17:48.380 --> 00:17:50.530
Thanks a lot for being
a part of the alpha

00:17:50.530 --> 00:17:53.050
and for the tremendous
feedback you've provided.

00:17:53.050 --> 00:17:55.030
It's great to see how
you are leveraging

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:57.610
the power of containers
to run your own binary

00:17:57.610 --> 00:18:02.210
in the free auto scale
environment that is Cloud Run.

00:18:02.210 --> 00:18:02.980
OK.

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:07.140
So let's go a bit about
how we get to a container.

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:08.640
Maybe that's of use
for some of you,

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:10.360
but let's just go over this.

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:13.780
The first thing is to
actually code your service.

00:18:13.780 --> 00:18:16.750
On Cloud Run, we have
a few requirements

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:22.640
for you, not a lot, but
very important ones.

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:25.960
So we call that the
container runtime contract.

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:29.380
Any container that
meets those requirements

00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:33.880
will be able to be deployed
in auto scale on Cloud Run.

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:39.010
The first one is that the
request and the Pub/Sub events

00:18:39.010 --> 00:18:41.260
and any other
asynchronous task are

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:45.880
going to be delivered to your
container via the HTTP protocol

00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:47.480
on a given port.

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:49.700
So from your side
as a developer,

00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:53.680
you have listen for HTTP
requests on that port.

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:56.920
You know how to do that, right?

00:18:56.920 --> 00:19:00.490
The other very important
point is that your container

00:19:00.490 --> 00:19:03.730
should not rely on local state.

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:06.040
Of course, you can use
persistent storage,

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:09.790
like databases and blob storage.

00:19:09.790 --> 00:19:13.390
But inside a container, you can
only rely on temporary states.

00:19:13.390 --> 00:19:15.550
The things that you create,
because of the nature

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:17.620
of the autoscaling of
Cloud Run, will not

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:22.260
be here necessarily the next
time we invoke your container.

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:24.080
And so to store a
state, of course,

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:25.390
Google Cloud has many options.

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:30.820
Filestore, Cloud
Storage, BigQuery.

00:19:30.820 --> 00:19:35.170
So once you've built your app
that meets those requirements,

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:38.750
you can package it up
into a container image.

00:19:38.750 --> 00:19:41.060
So we call that the build phase.

00:19:41.060 --> 00:19:44.980
So it's literally we take all
of those files, run a command,

00:19:44.980 --> 00:19:48.050
and end up with a
container image.

00:19:48.050 --> 00:19:52.990
To do that, the obvious tool
that everybody knows about

00:19:52.990 --> 00:19:54.540
is Docker.

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:56.620
Docker Build,
Docker Push, this is

00:19:56.620 --> 00:20:00.670
what will transform your local
source into a container image

00:20:00.670 --> 00:20:03.490
and push it to the
Container Registry.

00:20:03.490 --> 00:20:06.010
The Google Cloud has
another product for you.

00:20:06.010 --> 00:20:08.740
That is Cloud Build.

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:10.480
And Cloud Build
allows you to do just

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:13.390
that, build your container,
except that this time,

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:14.860
it is doing it in the cloud.

00:20:14.860 --> 00:20:17.920
So you don't even need to have
the tooling installed locally.

00:20:17.920 --> 00:20:21.250
And once again, like
any serverless product,

00:20:21.250 --> 00:20:22.265
you pay per usage.

00:20:22.265 --> 00:20:23.890
So when you don't
build, you don't pay.

00:20:23.890 --> 00:20:27.190
And you just pay for
the time of your builds.

00:20:27.190 --> 00:20:30.100
So Cloud Build can
build local containers,

00:20:30.100 --> 00:20:33.130
can do even more complex
builds, and can also

00:20:33.130 --> 00:20:35.620
help you automatically
deploy your container

00:20:35.620 --> 00:20:38.550
image to Cloud Run.

00:20:38.550 --> 00:20:41.280
And lastly, we should
mention that containers

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:44.130
are a very good fit with
continuous integration

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:45.660
and continuous delivery.

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:50.130
For example, you will build
your container once, test it

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:54.480
locally, test it on your
continuous testing system,

00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:56.550
push it to staging, making
sure that everything

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:59.870
is good on staging, and then
take that exact same container

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:02.480
and deploy to production.

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:05.332
The container stays the same,
because that's very important.

00:21:05.332 --> 00:21:07.290
The thing that you tested
is actually the thing

00:21:07.290 --> 00:21:08.780
that goes into production.

00:21:08.780 --> 00:21:12.450
So containers are a
very good tool for that.

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:14.050
And as I said,
Cloud Build can help

00:21:14.050 --> 00:21:15.640
you achieve the
continuous integration

00:21:15.640 --> 00:21:16.973
and continuous delivery.

00:21:16.973 --> 00:21:18.640
But of course, you
can use whatever tool

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:21.060
you want to do that.

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:24.460
And the last step
is to run things.

00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:28.370
So here we go from one container
image in Google Cloud Container

00:21:28.370 --> 00:21:30.830
Registry, and with
one simple command

00:21:30.830 --> 00:21:35.210
or one click of a button,
we deploy that to Cloud Run.

00:21:35.210 --> 00:21:37.220
On Cloud Run, as
you've seen, you

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:40.870
get a stable and secured
HTTPS URL that you can decide

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:43.820
or not to expose
to the internet.

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:48.690
And this happens, as you've
seen, in just a few seconds.

00:21:48.690 --> 00:21:51.800
So the demo idea was really
a hello world container.

00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:56.260
I want to go over those
steps with a real use case.

00:21:56.260 --> 00:21:57.950
Let's switch to the
demo screen, please.

00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:01.650
Yes.

00:22:01.650 --> 00:22:02.150
OK.

00:22:02.150 --> 00:22:05.930
So what I have here is
just a bit of Python code

00:22:05.930 --> 00:22:10.890
that is listening for
incoming HTTP requests.

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:16.080
And then it's
invoking OpenOffice,

00:22:16.080 --> 00:22:19.020
because what Microsoft is doing
is it's converting Microsoft

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:21.030
Word documents into PDFs.

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:24.090
And to do that we just call
LibreOffice to get it together

00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:25.980
as a PDF.

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:30.510
There is another very important
file in my web service here.

00:22:30.510 --> 00:22:33.570
As you might know,
it's named Docker file.

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:35.670
If you don't know
this, don't be scared.

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:36.670
It's super simple.

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:40.480
Let's take a look, like
literally 16 lines.

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:46.110
The first line is me using
what we call a base image.

00:22:46.110 --> 00:22:48.940
The base image will
contain already

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:52.810
all of the packages and tools
to run that particular language.

00:22:52.810 --> 00:22:55.810
In our case, it's the
official Python base image

00:22:55.810 --> 00:22:58.200
maintained by the
Python community,

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:01.150
or maybe even the
Python maintainer.

00:23:01.150 --> 00:23:05.170
We also have a line where
we install OpenOffice,

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:07.910
because as you remember,
we invoked OpenOffice.

00:23:07.910 --> 00:23:11.860
So we first have to install it
from the regular Alpine package

00:23:11.860 --> 00:23:13.970
manager.

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:16.517
And the last step is to
specify your start command.

00:23:16.517 --> 00:23:17.600
That's what we have to do.

00:23:17.600 --> 00:23:21.950
Start command, that is how
to actually start our code.

00:23:21.950 --> 00:23:24.320
So as you remember,
the next step

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:26.500
is to build that into
a container image.

00:23:26.500 --> 00:23:28.000
I could use any tool.

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:33.760
But I'm going to use Cloud
Build, gcloud build submit.

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:36.350
I give you the tag.

00:23:36.350 --> 00:23:40.175
And here I directly point to
a Google container registery.

00:23:45.620 --> 00:23:47.285
Oh, there is a typo.

00:23:47.285 --> 00:23:47.785
Submit.

00:23:52.690 --> 00:23:53.190
OK.

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:58.500
So what's happening here, GCloud
is uploading my local source,

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:01.890
and then triggering a remote
build using Cloud Build.

00:24:01.890 --> 00:24:06.360
Because OpenOffice is quite
a big software, quite old,

00:24:06.360 --> 00:24:07.980
it's probably in Java.

00:24:07.980 --> 00:24:09.780
It has a lot of dependencies.

00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:13.410
Because of all of that, it can
take maybe up to two or three

00:24:13.410 --> 00:24:14.560
minutes to build.

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:15.930
So I will spare you this.

00:24:15.930 --> 00:24:17.872
I actually built
it before the demo.

00:24:17.872 --> 00:24:19.080
So let's go to the next step.

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:28.930
The next step is to
deploy that to Cloud Run.

00:24:28.930 --> 00:24:32.770
You have seen the Cloud Console
UI, where you can simply

00:24:32.770 --> 00:24:34.450
open a form and deploy.

00:24:34.450 --> 00:24:37.060
Here I'm going to use
the command line, which

00:24:37.060 --> 00:24:43.130
is gcloud beta, because we are
still in beta, run, deploy.

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:44.470
Then I pass an image.

00:24:44.470 --> 00:24:48.550
So as I told you, it's in
Google Cloud Container Registry.

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:51.280
Run-intro is our product name.

00:24:51.280 --> 00:24:55.780
And the image that I built
before is PDF-Service.

00:24:55.780 --> 00:24:57.920
Let's hit Enter.

00:24:57.920 --> 00:24:59.430
Without more
configuration, GCloud

00:24:59.430 --> 00:25:01.770
is going to ask me, where
do you want to deploy?

00:25:01.770 --> 00:25:05.120
And yes, Thomas, today it's only
US Central 1, but stay tuned.

00:25:05.120 --> 00:25:08.230
The other regions are coming.

00:25:08.230 --> 00:25:10.280
And it's suggesting
for me a service name.

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:11.350
Well, sounds good.

00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:17.250
And it's asking me,
do I want to allow

00:25:17.250 --> 00:25:20.290
unauthenticated
invocation to my service?

00:25:20.290 --> 00:25:23.250
This means, do I allow anybody
to invoke that service?

00:25:23.250 --> 00:25:25.380
And for the sake
of the demo, I'm

00:25:25.380 --> 00:25:29.370
going to say yes to show
it to you in my browser.

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:31.980
Right here, same as
before, Cloud Run

00:25:31.980 --> 00:25:34.530
is importing your
container image.

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:37.080
It's creating a
revision under the hood.

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:39.083
Right now it's performing
a health check.

00:25:39.083 --> 00:25:41.250
So that means it's making
sure that my container can

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:43.500
start and listen on
the appropriate port.

00:25:43.500 --> 00:25:47.550
You remember it listens for
HTTP requests on the port,

00:25:47.550 --> 00:25:48.580
on the given port.

00:25:48.580 --> 00:25:50.070
And we are done.

00:25:50.070 --> 00:25:54.000
We get-- let me zoom
out a little bit.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:56.970
We get a stable URL under HTTPS.

00:25:56.970 --> 00:25:59.870
Let's open it.

00:25:59.870 --> 00:26:00.370
OK.

00:26:00.370 --> 00:26:04.030
So this microservice
converts DOCs to PDFs.

00:26:04.030 --> 00:26:08.767
Let's give it to a DOC.

00:26:08.767 --> 00:26:12.120
Upload it.

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:15.270
So here my DOC is uploaded
to my cloud service.

00:26:15.270 --> 00:26:19.590
Under the hood, Cloud Run
is invoking OpenOffice

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:21.930
to convert that DOC into a PDF.

00:26:21.930 --> 00:26:23.400
And we get back a PDF.

00:26:23.400 --> 00:26:26.711
So this is the PDF that
I got back from Chrome--

00:26:26.711 --> 00:26:27.336
from Cloud Run.

00:26:27.336 --> 00:26:30.360
And I view it in Chrome.

00:26:30.360 --> 00:26:31.110
So that's it.

00:26:31.110 --> 00:26:36.300
Importantly here, I only had
to pay when my service was

00:26:36.300 --> 00:26:37.980
actually processing my request.

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:39.840
Right now it's scaled to zero.

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:41.040
I don't pay anything.

00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:44.520
It's only when I send a document
and when I process a document

00:26:44.520 --> 00:26:47.673
that I actually am charged
for the resource I'm using

00:26:47.673 --> 00:26:48.840
during that processing time.

00:26:52.070 --> 00:26:54.390
Now I will hand it over to Ryan.

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:56.130
And can we switch
back to the slides

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:59.490
to tell you about how
this is serverless.

00:26:59.490 --> 00:27:01.270
RYAN GREGG: I'm back.

00:27:01.270 --> 00:27:03.750
So that was a really
cool demo of how easy

00:27:03.750 --> 00:27:07.650
it is to take a container,
actually even take Python code,

00:27:07.650 --> 00:27:11.790
build it into a container,
deploy it using--

00:27:11.790 --> 00:27:13.410
I don't want to call it old.

00:27:13.410 --> 00:27:14.850
I'll call it legacy--

00:27:14.850 --> 00:27:16.238
vintage software

00:27:16.238 --> 00:27:17.280
STEREN GIANNINI: Vintage.

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:19.030
RYAN GREGG: That's now
running serverless.

00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:21.900
But what does it mean
by natively serverless?

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:23.760
How do we think
about how serverless

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:26.310
and the principles
that I defined earlier

00:27:26.310 --> 00:27:28.780
apply to Cloud Run?

00:27:28.780 --> 00:27:32.240
So first of all,
in the old world,

00:27:32.240 --> 00:27:35.730
if you were doing this on,
say, Compute Engine or even

00:27:35.730 --> 00:27:38.910
Kubernetes Engine, you would
have to provision servers.

00:27:38.910 --> 00:27:41.820
You'd have to think about
how to scale your workload,

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:44.610
and how many servers you might
need to be able to transform

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:47.262
thousands of PDFs on demand.

00:27:47.262 --> 00:27:48.720
And really, what
that would mean is

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:50.040
that you would have
to overprovision

00:27:50.040 --> 00:27:52.230
the number of servers that
you bought, because you wanted

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:54.720
to make sure that there was
enough capacity to handle all

00:27:54.720 --> 00:27:57.293
of the load that was coming in.

00:27:57.293 --> 00:27:58.710
The great thing
about Cloud Run is

00:27:58.710 --> 00:28:00.585
that you get to do all
of that without having

00:28:00.585 --> 00:28:02.130
to worry about any of it.

00:28:02.130 --> 00:28:03.880
Google takes care of it for you.

00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:05.550
So you as a developer
can just focus

00:28:05.550 --> 00:28:07.710
on the code that
matters, and let Google

00:28:07.710 --> 00:28:09.630
run all of the infrastructure.

00:28:09.630 --> 00:28:10.860
We give you a URL.

00:28:10.860 --> 00:28:13.230
We automatically
provision TLS certificates

00:28:13.230 --> 00:28:15.180
to give you a secure end point.

00:28:15.180 --> 00:28:18.293
We take care of the redundancy
and the automatic failover.

00:28:18.293 --> 00:28:19.710
We make sure that
everything keeps

00:28:19.710 --> 00:28:21.450
working so that
your application can

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:25.650
scale with you as more and more
users start using your service.

00:28:25.650 --> 00:28:29.340
And as Steren said,
it's all pay for use.

00:28:29.340 --> 00:28:32.280
So what does that
really look like?

00:28:32.280 --> 00:28:33.653
This is really simple, right?

00:28:33.653 --> 00:28:35.070
When you have no
requests, there's

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:37.260
no instance of your
application running.

00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:39.300
The container is put to sleep.

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:40.890
Later, when a request
comes in, we'll

00:28:40.890 --> 00:28:42.960
quickly spin up that
instance of the container

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:44.940
and start serving
traffic out of it.

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:46.670
And Cloud Run
supports concurrency,

00:28:46.670 --> 00:28:48.600
so we can serve
multiple requests out

00:28:48.600 --> 00:28:50.140
of that same container.

00:28:50.140 --> 00:28:52.740
As more load happens,
we'll create more instances

00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:55.230
of that container to handle
the load appropriately

00:28:55.230 --> 00:28:56.730
to keep the latency low.

00:28:56.730 --> 00:28:58.980
And then as the requests
start to trail off,

00:28:58.980 --> 00:29:01.700
we'll spin down instances
of that container.

00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:03.450
So you don't have to
keep paying for them.

00:29:03.450 --> 00:29:05.450
Actually, you don't have
to pay for them anyway.

00:29:05.450 --> 00:29:06.825
What does that
look like, though,

00:29:06.825 --> 00:29:08.160
on the monetary side, Steren?

00:29:08.160 --> 00:29:10.920
STEREN GIANNINI: Well, because
of these autoscaling mechanism,

00:29:10.920 --> 00:29:14.760
we are able to give you a pay
per use billing [INAUDIBLE]..

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:17.400
So that literally
means that you are only

00:29:17.400 --> 00:29:23.370
paying for the resources
that your service is using.

00:29:23.370 --> 00:29:27.750
In practice, that means paying
for exact CPU, exact memory,

00:29:27.750 --> 00:29:32.040
and number of requests
performed during the processing

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:36.810
of those requests down to an
increment of 100 milliseconds.

00:29:36.810 --> 00:29:43.890
Once the request is out, this
is zero on your bill for you.

00:29:43.890 --> 00:29:49.210
And so as Ryan said,
Cloud Run scales up.

00:29:49.210 --> 00:29:52.910
So we wanted to show
that to you on our demo.

00:29:52.910 --> 00:29:55.880
So let's switch to
the demo screen.

00:29:55.880 --> 00:29:58.600
What we have here is
a load testing tool

00:29:58.600 --> 00:30:02.650
that we send many requests
to or the container

00:30:02.650 --> 00:30:04.400
that we just deployed.

00:30:04.400 --> 00:30:09.180
So you remember
our container is--

00:30:09.180 --> 00:30:10.060
OK.

00:30:10.060 --> 00:30:12.330
Our container is
processing PDFs.

00:30:12.330 --> 00:30:15.240
So you remember that a PDF
processing time is not instant.

00:30:15.240 --> 00:30:18.570
It takes a few seconds
to download the PDF

00:30:18.570 --> 00:30:20.560
and then transform it.

00:30:20.560 --> 00:30:22.800
Download the DOC and
transform it into a PDF

00:30:22.800 --> 00:30:24.750
by invoking OpenOffice.

00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:27.980
So what you see here, I
asked the load testing tool

00:30:27.980 --> 00:30:32.360
to transform 1,000
PDFs in one minute.

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:37.610
And here what you see is the
scale up of Cloud Run, which

00:30:37.610 --> 00:30:40.800
OpenOffice being a
quite big software,

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:42.370
it takes some time to load.

00:30:42.370 --> 00:30:46.410
So that's why we see
some higher latency.

00:30:46.410 --> 00:30:51.500
And then so here we have
processed maybe 500 documents

00:30:51.500 --> 00:30:54.580
in roughly 30 seconds.

00:30:54.580 --> 00:30:58.350
And overall, the
latency, OpenOffice

00:30:58.350 --> 00:31:02.910
takes 10 seconds to start
and process the PDFs.

00:31:02.910 --> 00:31:07.080
Going forward, this time will go
down, because once a request--

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:09.900
once a document
has been processed,

00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:12.990
Cloud Run is able to reuse
the run instance in order

00:31:12.990 --> 00:31:14.650
to process the next one.

00:31:14.650 --> 00:31:17.970
So of course, the
latency of the startup

00:31:17.970 --> 00:31:19.180
will take a bit more time.

00:31:19.180 --> 00:31:22.620
But once Cloud Run has
many run instances for you,

00:31:22.620 --> 00:31:25.360
over time things
will settle down.

00:31:25.360 --> 00:31:29.820
And here we process
the 900 Word documents

00:31:29.820 --> 00:31:33.360
that we are stored in Google
Cloud Storage in a minute.

00:31:33.360 --> 00:31:38.030
So if you're interested
in scaling performances,

00:31:38.030 --> 00:31:44.830
Jason in his breakout session
went up to 10,000 instances

00:31:44.830 --> 00:31:46.580
with his Cloud Run service.

00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:49.770
However, here I just showed
you sending some load

00:31:49.770 --> 00:31:54.030
on a real use case, which
is processing documents,

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:55.540
which takes many seconds.

00:31:55.540 --> 00:31:59.590
So that's why we haven't
seen that much high scaling.

00:31:59.590 --> 00:32:03.838
But overall, 1,000 PDFs in
one minute is quite good.

00:32:03.838 --> 00:32:05.380
RYAN GREGG: It's
pretty cool that you

00:32:05.380 --> 00:32:08.170
took a 2 gigabyte
container with OpenOffice

00:32:08.170 --> 00:32:09.585
and ran it serverless.

00:32:09.585 --> 00:32:11.710
STEREN GIANNINI: The
container is literally 2 gigs.

00:32:11.710 --> 00:32:14.017
And we looked at it.

00:32:14.017 --> 00:32:16.225
RYAN GREGG: So if we could
switch back to the slides.

00:32:18.800 --> 00:32:24.400
So that was a good demo about
how Cloud Run can autoscale.

00:32:24.400 --> 00:32:28.210
The third pillar of Cloud
Run is one experience

00:32:28.210 --> 00:32:32.110
where you want it, which is all
about portability and openness.

00:32:32.110 --> 00:32:33.760
That last pillar
of the developer

00:32:33.760 --> 00:32:36.200
experience that I
introduced at the beginning.

00:32:36.200 --> 00:32:38.380
And portability probably
means different things

00:32:38.380 --> 00:32:40.060
to different people in the room.

00:32:40.060 --> 00:32:44.200
So it might mean being able
to take your workload that you

00:32:44.200 --> 00:32:47.680
started on-prem and being
able to move it to the cloud.

00:32:47.680 --> 00:32:50.410
Or it could be about portability
of your own understandings

00:32:50.410 --> 00:32:52.833
and experiences as a developer.

00:32:52.833 --> 00:32:54.250
The great thing
about Cloud Run is

00:32:54.250 --> 00:32:56.125
that we wanted to make
sure that we satisfied

00:32:56.125 --> 00:32:57.433
both of those constraints.

00:32:57.433 --> 00:32:59.350
We wanted to make sure
that if a developer had

00:32:59.350 --> 00:33:01.767
to learn something new to use
this platform, that it could

00:33:01.767 --> 00:33:04.940
be reused on every project they
would work on in the future,

00:33:04.940 --> 00:33:08.680
that those skills could
transfer again and again.

00:33:08.680 --> 00:33:10.750
And Cloud Run has
a great promise.

00:33:10.750 --> 00:33:12.940
But I'm going to take
a side note here,

00:33:12.940 --> 00:33:15.340
because I've talked
about containers.

00:33:15.340 --> 00:33:17.338
And I've talked
about portability.

00:33:17.338 --> 00:33:18.880
And if you know
Google at all, you're

00:33:18.880 --> 00:33:22.720
probably thinking, like,
hey, what about Kubernetes?

00:33:22.720 --> 00:33:24.320
What about Kubernetes Engine?

00:33:24.320 --> 00:33:25.778
That's what I think
of when I think

00:33:25.778 --> 00:33:27.580
of containers and portability.

00:33:27.580 --> 00:33:29.920
And you'd be right.

00:33:29.920 --> 00:33:32.350
Kubernetes is a very
portable platform

00:33:32.350 --> 00:33:35.620
that supports running
containers really well today.

00:33:35.620 --> 00:33:38.730
However, Kubernetes is really
fantastic for the operator,

00:33:38.730 --> 00:33:40.780
but doesn't do a whole
lot for the developer.

00:33:40.780 --> 00:33:43.270
You still have to think about
how to scale your application

00:33:43.270 --> 00:33:44.920
and how to do the
network programming,

00:33:44.920 --> 00:33:47.470
how to manage all of the details
of how your container will

00:33:47.470 --> 00:33:49.790
run on Kubernetes.

00:33:49.790 --> 00:33:53.620
And as Kelsey said, Kubernetes
is a great starting place.

00:33:53.620 --> 00:33:57.710
It's a great platform for
building other platforms.

00:33:57.710 --> 00:33:59.510
It's not the end game.

00:33:59.510 --> 00:34:01.510
This is where
Cloud Run comes in.

00:34:01.510 --> 00:34:05.560
Cloud Run and its component,
or its sister component,

00:34:05.560 --> 00:34:09.250
Cloud Run on GKE, provides a
serverless experience anywhere

00:34:09.250 --> 00:34:10.690
that you need it to be.

00:34:10.690 --> 00:34:13.150
So like the Cloud
Run managed service

00:34:13.150 --> 00:34:15.670
that Steren was showing
you, Cloud Run on GKE

00:34:15.670 --> 00:34:17.889
gives you many of
the same benefits.

00:34:17.889 --> 00:34:19.540
The same flexibility
and the control

00:34:19.540 --> 00:34:22.449
to run a container,
any container,

00:34:22.449 --> 00:34:24.070
on your Kubernetes cluster.

00:34:24.070 --> 00:34:26.659
You get the same
autoscaling benefits,

00:34:26.659 --> 00:34:28.870
including scaling to zero.

00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:32.270
You can even take advantage
of GKE specific features.

00:34:32.270 --> 00:34:34.960
So you can use
custom machine types.

00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:38.463
You can ask for incredibly
high amounts of memory.

00:34:38.463 --> 00:34:39.880
You can use hardware
accelerators,

00:34:39.880 --> 00:34:41.739
like GPUs and TPUs.

00:34:41.739 --> 00:34:45.310
And you can have access
to the VPC networking.

00:34:45.310 --> 00:34:47.560
All of this lets
you use the benefits

00:34:47.560 --> 00:34:51.290
of Cloud Run on your existing
Kubernetes investment.

00:34:51.290 --> 00:34:55.570
So you can be fully serverless
and run on a regular house--

00:34:55.570 --> 00:34:58.760
excuse me-- the regular
fully managed Cloud Run.

00:34:58.760 --> 00:35:00.760
Or you can have that same
developer experience,

00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:03.640
the same tooling, the
same API, and run it

00:35:03.640 --> 00:35:07.610
inside your Kubernetes Engine
Cluster with Cloud Run on GKE.

00:35:07.610 --> 00:35:09.520
And now to tell you
about why this truly

00:35:09.520 --> 00:35:11.320
matters to our
customers, I'd like

00:35:11.320 --> 00:35:13.840
to invite Guillaume from
Birdz up to the stage

00:35:13.840 --> 00:35:16.104
to tell you about his use case.

00:35:16.104 --> 00:35:20.000
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:21.687 --> 00:35:23.270
GUILLAUME BLAQUIERE:
Hello, everybody.

00:35:23.270 --> 00:35:25.250
I'm Guillaume Blaquiere.

00:35:25.250 --> 00:35:27.430
I'm the data delivery
team leader at Birdz.

00:35:27.430 --> 00:35:31.710
And Birdz is a branch
company of Veolia.

00:35:31.710 --> 00:35:33.650
Veolia is a major
French company.

00:35:33.650 --> 00:35:41.330
We have 170,000 employees
over 50 countries.

00:35:41.330 --> 00:35:46.560
The core businesses of Veolia
are water, waste, and energy.

00:35:46.560 --> 00:35:49.665
Birdz, it's the branch
company of Veolia.

00:35:49.665 --> 00:35:54.050
It specializes in IoT,
which designs devices.

00:35:54.050 --> 00:35:59.690
I have to deploy and
configure devices on field.

00:35:59.690 --> 00:36:02.270
We process the data.

00:36:02.270 --> 00:36:07.220
We store them, and we divert
services to our customer.

00:36:07.220 --> 00:36:11.360
And all of this works on a
local network with our legacy

00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:15.360
protocol, but we also have the
new ones, like Sigfox, Lorawan,

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:17.690
and NB-IoT.

00:36:17.690 --> 00:36:21.380
The main business of Birdz
are smart water meters,

00:36:21.380 --> 00:36:25.820
which represents
90% of our assets

00:36:25.820 --> 00:36:30.380
on fields with devices
designed to live up

00:36:30.380 --> 00:36:33.880
to 15 years on lithium battery.

00:36:33.880 --> 00:36:36.760
These devices have to
be deployed on feed

00:36:36.760 --> 00:36:39.080
by a plumber with
special training

00:36:39.080 --> 00:36:42.720
to plug and to
configure the devices.

00:36:42.720 --> 00:36:46.910
These steps take times,
and thus takes money,

00:36:46.910 --> 00:36:50.840
because a small [INAUDIBLE]
is more than 10,000 water

00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:53.370
meters to deploy.

00:36:53.370 --> 00:36:54.950
That's why our
customers don't want

00:36:54.950 --> 00:36:58.610
to deploy devices
on each contract,

00:36:58.610 --> 00:37:04.130
and want to reuse Birdz
devices all the life long.

00:37:04.130 --> 00:37:07.730
Even if it's not Birdz
or Veolia as contractor.

00:37:07.730 --> 00:37:15.020
And thus they also want to
decode the Birdz messages

00:37:15.020 --> 00:37:21.250
on the home at the end of the
contract with Birdz or Veolia.

00:37:21.250 --> 00:37:25.070
That's why we
designed this solution

00:37:25.070 --> 00:37:30.680
where an IoT operator sends
us messages through Webhook.

00:37:30.680 --> 00:37:36.100
We push them to a pub/sub to
absorb some peak of charge.

00:37:36.100 --> 00:37:41.000
Pub/sub pushes the
messages to the decoder

00:37:41.000 --> 00:37:44.360
to duplicate, decode,
and valorize the data,

00:37:44.360 --> 00:37:46.370
and store them into bitcoin.

00:37:46.370 --> 00:37:49.370
[INAUDIBLE] a scheduler
triggers a container

00:37:49.370 --> 00:37:54.230
to extract the data
from bitcoin and to load

00:37:54.230 --> 00:37:56.840
them-- to load it in
the database for serving

00:37:56.840 --> 00:37:59.580
our websites.

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:03.480
The customer wants to post
and to process the decoding

00:38:03.480 --> 00:38:05.660
by themselves, and to
have the capability

00:38:05.660 --> 00:38:11.840
to deploy on the
cluster with Knative.

00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:17.000
This part, it could be on
GCP, or on the cloud provider,

00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:21.150
or even on-premise.

00:38:21.150 --> 00:38:26.660
With GCP, we have two solutions
to deploy our container.

00:38:26.660 --> 00:38:28.380
And therefore, the
message acquisition

00:38:28.380 --> 00:38:32.920
is quite simple, because it's
independent, simple, and light.

00:38:32.920 --> 00:38:35.975
And we have to absorb
some peak of charge.

00:38:35.975 --> 00:38:37.350
That's why we
choose to deploy it

00:38:37.350 --> 00:38:40.970
on the fully managed solution.

00:38:40.970 --> 00:38:44.100
On the other hand,
the message processing

00:38:44.100 --> 00:38:47.100
has to access on-prem
data through a VPN,

00:38:47.100 --> 00:38:49.650
and just through a VPC.

00:38:49.650 --> 00:38:51.810
And the custom VPC are
not yet implemented

00:38:51.810 --> 00:38:54.270
in a free manager solution.

00:38:54.270 --> 00:38:56.720
And we have to deploy it on GKE.

00:38:56.720 --> 00:39:02.370
Morever, this decoding part has
to access stateful resources,

00:39:02.370 --> 00:39:06.460
like a [INAUDIBLE]
or persistent volume.

00:39:06.460 --> 00:39:09.260
That's why GKE is the best case.

00:39:09.260 --> 00:39:11.820
The third part, the
data preparation

00:39:11.820 --> 00:39:14.700
is quite difficult to choose.

00:39:14.700 --> 00:39:17.130
Indeed, it's an infrequent
call, and it can be quick.

00:39:17.130 --> 00:39:20.310
It's just few data
and few customers,

00:39:20.310 --> 00:39:23.730
but it can be too long for
the fully-managed solution.

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:27.760
It is a [INAUDIBLE]
of customers.

00:39:27.760 --> 00:39:31.200
To date, it's not a problem,
because with the same code,

00:39:31.200 --> 00:39:37.260
the same container, the same
UI, or the same common name,

00:39:37.260 --> 00:39:41.040
if you want, it's really easy
to move from one solution

00:39:41.040 --> 00:39:42.060
to another one.

00:39:42.060 --> 00:39:48.410
You only need to specify or not
the cluster and the solution

00:39:48.410 --> 00:39:50.040
is deployed on--

00:39:50.040 --> 00:39:52.370
one solution or the other one.

00:39:52.370 --> 00:39:57.950
It's very easy and really good.

00:39:57.950 --> 00:40:00.574
Thanks, Steren and
Ryan, for that.

00:40:00.574 --> 00:40:01.133
[APPLAUSE]

